  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Experimental Affinities in Music shows early scholarly uptake: OpenAlex records three citations, spread across 2019, 2020, and 2022. The citing record is small but notably varied, reaching an article, a dissertation, and a book chapter, which suggests the book is being used across different kinds of musicological and research-oriented work. The book is also available through open-access channels and is held in library collections, supporting discoverability and reuse.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Paulo de Assis’s Experimental Affinities in Music, published by Leuven University Press in 2015, has begun to register as a reference point for later scholarship on experimental practice, improvisation, and musicological reflection. The record shows three citations in OpenAlex, appearing over several years rather than in a single burst. That pattern indicates modest but continuing uptake, with the book being drawn on in work that engages experimental music, performance, and historical or theoretical approaches to composition.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The citing literature is diverse in format. It includes an article in Eighteenth Century Music, a dissertation from the University of São Paulo, and a book chapter published in a research context. This mix suggests that the book’s arguments have resonance beyond one publication type and are being used in both advanced student research and disciplinary scholarship. The associated concepts in the citing works point especially toward musicology, improvisation, performance, and broader experimental approaches.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The book’s reach is also strengthened by its availability: OpenAIRE identifies it as hybrid open access with multiple open instances, and library records show holdings in music, arts, philosophy and aesthetics, performance, and experimental methods. Those channels do not themselves demonstrate citation impact, but they do improve the conditions for readership and classroom or research use.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Funding support from the European Research Council adds another layer of scholarly context, indicating that the project sat within a funded research environment. Overall, the evidence points to a work with a focused but credible scholarly footprint: early citation activity, uptake across different research forms, and broad disciplinary discoverability in the fields most closely aligned with its themes.</p>",
